Γαλλίων|Gall�wn|callizn|Gallio = "One who lives on milk"<br />1) Junius Annaeus Gallio, the Roman proconsul of Achaia when Paul was at Corinth, 53 A.D., under the emperor Claudius. Act 18:12. He was brother to Jucius Annaeus Seneca, the philosopher. Jerome in the Chronicle of Eusebius says that he committed suicide in 65 A.D. Winer thinks he was put to death by Nero.<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun proper masculine|<b>G1058</b><br />Of Latin origin; <i>Gallion</i> (that is, <i>Gallio</i>), a Roman officer: - Gallio<br />|1058|
Γαμαλιήλ|Gamali�l|camaligl|Gamaliel = "my recompenser is God"<br />1) a Pharisee and celebrated doctor of the law, who gave prudent worldly advice in the Sanhedrin respecting the treatment of the followers of Jesus of Nazareth. Act 5:34 ff. (A.D.29.) We learn from Act 22:3 that he was the preceptor of Paul. He is generally identified with the very celebrated Jewish doctor Gamaliel, grandson of Hillel, and who is referred to as authority in the Jewish Mishna.<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun proper masculine|<b>G1059</b><br />Of Hebrew origin [H1583]; <i>Gamaliel</i> (that is, <i>Gamliel</i>), an Israelite: - Gamaliel.<br />|1059|
γαμέω|gam�w|camez|1) to lead in marriage, take to wife<br />1a) to get married, to marry<br />1b) to give one's self in marriage<br />2) to give a daughter in marriage<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 1:648, 111|<b>G1060</b><br />From G1062; to <i>wed</i> (of either sex): - marry (a wife).<br />|1060|
