δέχομαι|d�comai|dexpmai|1) to take with the hand<br />1a) to take hold of, take up<br />2) to take up, receive<br />2a) used of a place receiving one<br />2b) to receive or grant access to, a visitor, not to refuse intercourse or friendship<br />2b1) to receive hospitality<br />2b2) to receive into one's family to bring up or educate<br />2c) of the thing offered in speaking, teaching, instructing<br />2c1) to receive favourably, give ear to, embrace, make one's own, approve, not to reject<br />2d) to receive. i.e. to take upon one's self, sustain, bear, endure<br />3) to receive, get<br />3a) to learn<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 2:50, 146|<b>G1209</b><br />Middle voice of a primary verb; to <i>receive</i> (in various applications, literally or figuratively): - accept, receive, take. Compare G2983.<br />|1209|
δέω|d�w|dez|1) to bind tie, fasten<br />1a) to bind, fasten with chains, to throw into chains<br />1b) metaphorically<br />1b1) Satan is said to bind a woman bent together by means of a demon, as his messenger, taking possession of the woman and preventing her from standing upright<br />1b2) to bind, put under obligation, of the law, duty etc.<br />1b2a) to be bound to one, a wife, a husband<br />1b3) to forbid, prohibit, declare to be illicit<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 2:60, 148|<b>G1210</b><br />A primary verb; to <i>bind</i> (in various applications, literally or figuratively): - bind, be in bonds, knit, tie, wind. See also G1163, G1189.<br />|1210|
δή|d�|dg|1) now, then, verily, in truth, really, surely, certainly<br />2) forthwith, at once<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> particle|<b>G1211</b><br />Probably akin to G1161; a particle of emphasis or explicitness; <i>now</i>, <i>then</i>, etc.: - also, and, doubtless, now, therefore.<br />|1211|
δῆλος|d�loj|dglps|1) clear, evident, manifest<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adjective|<b>G1212</b><br />Of uncertain derivation; <i>clear:</i> - + bewray, certain, evident, manifest.<br />|1212|
δηλόω|dhl�w|dglpz|1) to make manifest<br />2) to make known by relating, to declare<br />3) to give one to understand, to indicate, signify<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 2:61, 148|<b>G1213</b><br />From G1212; to <i>make plain</i> (by words): - declare, shew, signify.<br />|1213|
Δημᾶς|Dhm�j|dgmas|Demas = "governor of the people"<br />1) a companion of Paul, who deserted the apostle when he was a prisoner at Rome and returned to Thessalonica<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun proper masculine|<b>G1214</b><br />Probably for G1216; <i>Demas</i>, a Christian: - Demas.<br />|1214|
δημηγορέω|dhmhgor�w|dgmgcprez|1) to address a public assembly, make a speech to the people<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb|<b>G1215</b><br />From a compound of G1218 and G58; to <i>be a people gatherer</i>, that is, to <i>address</i> a public assembly: - make an oration.<br />|1215|
Δημήτριος|Dhm�trioj|dgmgtrips|Demetrius = "belonging to Ceres"<br />1) a heathen silversmith at Ephesus<br />2) a certain Christian, 3Jo 12<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun proper masculine|<b>G1216</b><br />From <span class="uni">Δημήτηρ</span> D&ecirc;m&ecirc;t&ecirc;r (<i>Ceres</i>); <i>Demetrius</i>, the name of an Ephesian and of a Christian: - Demetrius.<br />|1216|
δημιουργός|dhmiourg�j|dgmipurcps|1) a workman for the public<br />2) the author of any work, an artisan, framer, builder<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 2:62, 149|<b>G1217</b><br />From G1218 and G2041; a <i>worker</i> for the <i>people</i>, that is, <i>mechanic</i> (spoken of the <i>Creator</i>): - maker.<br />|1217|
δῆμος|d�moj|dgmps|1) the people, the mass of people assembled in a public place<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 2:63, 149|<b>G1218</b><br />From G1210; the <i>public</i> (as <i>bound</i> together socially): - people.<br />|1218|
δημόσιος|dhm�sioj|dgmpsips|1) belonging to the people or state, public<br />2) publicly, in public places, in full view of all<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adjective|<b>G1219</b><br />From G1218; <i>public</i>; (feminine singular dative as adverb) <i>in public:</i> - common, openly, publickly.<br />|1219|
δηνάριον|dhn�rion|dgnaripn|denarius = "containing ten"<br />1) A Roman silver coin in NT time. It took its name from it being equal to ten "asses", a number after 217 B.C. increased to sixteen (about 3.898 grams or .1375 oz.). It was the principal silver coin of the Roman empire. From the parable of the labourers in the vineyard, it would seem that a denarius was then the ordinary pay for a day's wages. (Mat 20:2-13)<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter|<b>G1220</b><br />Of Latin origin; a <i>denarius</i> (or <i>ten asses</i>): - pence, penny [-worth].<br />|1220|
δήποτε|d�pote|dgqpte|1) now at length<br />2) at any time<br />3) at last<br />4) just exactly<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adverb|<b>G1221</b><br />From G1211 and G4218; a particle of generalization; <i>indeed</i>, <i>at any time:</i> - (what-) soever.<br />|1221|
δήπου|d�pou|dgqpu|1) it is used when something is affirmed in a slightly ironical manner, as if with an pretence of uncertainty<br />1a) perhaps<br />1b) doubtless<br />1c) verily<br />1d) truly<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adverb|<b>G1222</b><br />From G1211 and G4225; a particle of asseveration; <i>indeed doubtless:</i> - verily.<br />|1222|
διά|di�|dia|1) through<br />1a) of place<br />1a1) with<br />1a2) in<br />1b) of time<br />1b1) throughout<br />1b2) during<br />1c) of means<br />1c1) by<br />1c2) by the means of<br />2) through<br />2a) the ground or reason by which something is or is not done<br />2a1) by reason of<br />2a2) on account of<br />2a3) because of for this reason<br />2a4) therefore<br />2a5) on this account<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> preposition<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 2:65, 149|<b>G1223</b><br />A primary preposition denoting the <i>channel</i> of an act; <i>through</i> (in very wide applications, local, causal or occasional). In composition it retains the same general import: - after, always, among, at, to avoid, because of (that), briefly, by, for (cause) . . . fore, from, in, by occasion of, of, by reason of, for sake, that, thereby, therefore, X though, through (-out), to, wherefore, with (-in). In composition it retains the same general import.<br />|1223|
διαβαίνω|diaba�nw|diabainz|1) to pass through, cross over<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb|<b>G1224</b><br />From G1223 and the base of G939; to <i>cross:</i> - come over, pass (through).<br />|1224|
διαβάλλω|diab�llw|diaballz|1) to throw over or across, to send over<br />2) to traduce, calumniate, slander, accuse, defame<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 2:71, 150|<b>G1225</b><br />From G1223 and G906; (figuratively) to <i>traduce:</i> - accuse.<br />|1225|
διαβεβαιόομαι|diabebai�omai|diabebaippmai|1) to affirm strongly, assert confidently<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb|<b>G1226</b><br />Middle voice of a compound of G1223 and G950; to <i>confirm thoroughly</i> (by words), that is, <i>asseverate:</i> - affirm constantly.<br />|1226|
διαβλέπω|diabl�pw|diableqz|1) to look through, penetrate by vision<br />1a) to look fixedly, stare straight before one<br />1b) to see clearly<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb|<b>G1227</b><br />From G1223 and G991; to <i>look through</i>, that is, <i>recover</i> full <i>vision:</i> - see clearly.<br />|1227|
