θεάομαι|qe�omai|heapmai|1) to behold, look upon, view attentively, contemplate (often used of public shows)<br />1a) of important persons that are looked on with admiration<br />2) to view, take a view of<br />2a) in the sense of visiting, meeting with a person<br />3) to learn by looking, to see with the eyes, to perceive<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:315, 706|<b>G2300</b><br />A prolonged form of a primary verb; to <i>look</i> closely at, that is, (by implication) to <i>perceive</i> (literally or figuratively); by extension to <i>visit</i>: - behold, look (upon), see. Compare G3700.<br />|2300|
θεατρίζω|qeatr�zw|heatrifz|1) to bring upon the stage<br />2) to set forth as a spectacle, expose to contempt<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:42,*|<b>G2301</b><br />From G2302; to <i>expose as a spectacle:</i> - make a gazing stock.<br />|2301|
θέατρον|q�atron|heatrpn|1) a theatre, a place in which games and dramatic spectacles are exhibited, and public assemblies held (for the Greeks used the theatre also as a forum)<br />2) a public show<br />2a) metaphorically, a man who is exhibited to be gazed at and made sport of<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:42, 318|<b>G2302</b><br />From G2300; <i>a place for public show</i> ("theatre"), that is, general <i>audience room</i>; by implication a <i>show</i> itself (figuratively): - spectacle, theatre.<br />|2302|
θεῖον|qe�on|heipn|1) brimstone<br />1a) divine incense, because burning brimstone was regarded as having power to purify, and to ward off disease<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:122,*|<b>G2303</b><br />Probably neuter of G2304 (in its original sense of <i>flashing</i>); <i>sulphur:</i> - brimstone.<br />|2303|
