κενός|ken�j|kenps|1) empty, vain, devoid of truth<br />1a) of places, vessels, etc. which contain nothing<br />1b) of men<br />1b1) empty handed<br />1b2) without a gift<br />1c) metaphorically destitute of spiritual wealth, of one who boasts of his faith as a transcendent possession, yet is without the fruits of faith<br />1d) metaphorically of endeavours, labours, acts, which result in nothing, vain, fruitless, without effect<br />1d1) vain of no purpose<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adjective<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:659, 426|<b>G2756</b><br />Apparently a primary word; <i>empty</i> (literally or figuratively): - empty, (in) vain.<br />|2756|
κενοφωνία|kenofwn�a|kenpvznia|1) empty discussion, discussion of vain and useless matters<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine|<b>G2757</b><br />From a presumed compound of G2756 and G5456; <i>empty sounding</i>, that is, <i>fruitless discussion:</i> - vain.<br />|2757|
κενόω|ken�w|kenpz|1) to empty, make empty<br />1a) of Christ, he laid aside equality with or the form of God<br />2) to make void<br />2a) deprive of force, render vain, useless, of no effect<br />3) to make void<br />3b) cause a thing to be seen to be empty, hollow, false<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:661, 426|<b>G2758</b><br />From G2756; to <i>make empty</i>, that is, (figuratively) to <i>abase</i>, <i>neutralize</i>, <i>falsify:</i> - make (of none effect, of no reputation, void), be in vain.<br />|2758|
κέντρον|k�ntron|kentrpn|1) a sting, as that of bees, scorpions, locusts. Since animals wound by their sting and even cause death, Paul attributes death, personified as a sting, i.e. a deadly weapon<br />2) an iron goad, for urging on oxen, horses and other beasts of burden<br />2a) hence the proverb, "to kick against the goad", i.e. to offer vain and perilous or ruinous resistance<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:663, 427|<b>G2759</b><br />From <span class="uni">κεντέω</span> kente&ocirc; (to <i>prick</i>); a <i>point</i> ("centre"), that is, a <i>sting</i> (figuratively <i>poison</i>) or <i>goad</i> (figuratively divine <i>impulse</i>): - prick, sting.<br />|2759|
κεντυρίων|kentur�wn|kenturizn|1) centurion, an officer in the Roman army<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine|<b>G2760</b><br />Of Latin origin; a <i>centurion</i>, that is, <i>captain</i> of one hundred soldiers: - centurion.<br />|2760|
κενῶς|ken�j|kenzs|1) vainly, in vain<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adverb|<b>G2761</b><br />Adverb from G2756; <i>vainly</i>, that is, <i>to no purpose:</i> - in vain.<br />|2761|
κεραία|kera�a|keraia|1) a little horn<br />2) extremity, apex, point<br />2a) used by grammarians of the accents and diacritical points. Jesus used it of the little lines or projections, by which the Hebrew letters in other respects similar differ from one another; the meaning is, "not even the minutest part of the law shall perish".<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine|<b>G2762</b><br />Feminine of a presumed derivative of the base of G2768; something <i>horn like</i>, that is, (specifically) the <i>apex</i> of a Hebrew letter (figuratively the least <i>particle</i>): - tittle.<br />|2762|
κεραμεύς|kerame�j|kerameus|1) a potter<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine|<b>G2763</b><br />From G2766; a <i>potter:</i> - potter.<br />|2763|
κεραμικός|keramik�j|keramikps|1) of or belonging to a potter<br />2) made of clay, earthen<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adjective|<b>G2764</b><br />From G2766; <i>made of clay</i>, that is, <i>earthen:</i> - of a potter.<br />|2764|
κεράμιον|ker�mion|keramipn|1) an earthen vessel, a pot, jar<br />2) a jug or pitcher<br />3) a water pitcher<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter|<b>G2765</b><br />Neuter of a presumed derivative of G2766; an <i>earthenware</i> vessel, that is, <i>jar:</i> - pitcher.<br />|2765|
κέραμος|k�ramoj|keramps|1) clay, potter's earth<br />2) anything made of clay, earthen ware<br />3) a roofing tile<br />3a) the roof itself<br />3b) the phrase "through the roof", means through the door in the roof to which a ladder or stairway led up from the street (according to the Rabbis distinguish two ways of entering a house, "the way through the door" and "the way through the roof"<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine|<b>G2766</b><br />Probably from the base of G2767 (through the idea of <i>mixing</i> clay and water); <i>earthenware</i>, that is, a <i>tile</i> (by analogy a thin <i>roof</i> or <i>awning</i>): - tiling.<br />|2766|
κεράννυμι|ker�nnumi|kerannumi|1) to mix, mingle<br />2) to mix wine, water<br />3) to pour out for drinking.<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb|<b>G2767</b><br />A prolonged form of a more primary word <span class="uni">κεράω</span> kera&ocirc; (which is used in certain tenses); to <i>mingle</i>, that is, (by implication) to <i>pour</i> out (for drinking): - fill, pour out. Compare G3396.<br />|2767|
κέρας|k�raj|keras|1) a horn<br />1a) of animals<br />1b) since animals (especially bulls) defend themselves with their horns, the horn with the Hebrews (and other nations) is a symbol of strength and courage, and used as such in a variety of phrases<br />1b1) a mighty and valiant helper, the author of deliverance, of the Messiah<br />1c) a projecting extremity in a shape like a horn, a point, apex: as of an altar<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:669, 428|<b>G2768</b><br />From a primary word <span class="uni">κάρ</span> kar (the <i>hair</i> of the head); a <i>horn</i> (literally or figuratively): - horn.<br />|2768|
κεράτιον|ker�tion|keratipn|1) a little horn<br />2) the name of the fruit, Ceratonia silqua or carobtree (called also John's Bread [from the notion that its pods, which resemble those of the "locust", constituted the food of the Baptist]. This fruit was shaped like a horn and has a sweet taste; it was and is used not only for fattening swine, but as an article of food by the lower classes.<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter|<b>G2769</b><br />Neuter of a presumed derivative of G2768; something <i>horned</i>, that is, (specifically) the <i>pod</i> of the carob tree: - husk.<br />|2769|
κερδαίνω|kerda�nw|kerdainz|1) to gain, acquire, to get gain<br />2) metaphorically<br />2a) of gain arising from shunning or escaping from evil (where we say "to spare one's self", "be spared")<br />2b) to gain any one, i.e. to win him over to the kingdom of God, to gain one to faith in Christ<br />2c) to gain Christ's favour and fellowship<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:672, 428|<b>G2770</b><br />From G2771; to <i>gain</i> (literally or figuratively): - (get) gain, win.<br />|2770|
κέρδος|k�rdoj|kerdps|1) gain, advantage<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:672, 428|<b>G2771</b><br />Of uncertain affinity; <i>gain</i> (pecuniary or generally): - gain, lucre.<br />|2771|
κέρμα|k�rma|kerma|1) small pieces of money, small coin, change, money<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter|<b>G2772</b><br />From G2751; a <i>clipping</i> (<i>bit</i>), that is, (specifically) a <i>coin:</i> - money.<br />|2772|
κερματιστής|kermatist�j|kermatistgs|1) a money changer, money broker<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine|<b>G2773</b><br />From a derivative of G2772; a <i>handler of coins</i>, that is, <i>money broker:</i> - changer of money.<br />|2773|
κεφάλαιον|kef�laion|kevalaipn|1) the chief or main point, the principal thing<br />2) the pecuniary sum total of a reckoning, amount<br />2a) the principal, capital, as distinguished from the interest<br />2b) a sum of money, sum<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter|<b>G2774</b><br />Neuter of a derivative of G2776; a <i>principal</i> thing, that is, <i>main point</i>; specifically an <i>amount</i> (of money): - sum.<br />|2774|
κεφαλιόω|kefali�w|kevalipz|1) to bring under headings, to sum up, to summarise<br />2) to smite or wound in the head, to smite on the cheek<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb|<b>G2775</b><br />From the same as G2774; (specifically) to <i>strike on the head:</i> - wound in the head.<br />|2775|
κεφαλή|kefal�|kevalg|1) the head, both of men and often of animals. Since the loss of the head destroys life, this word is used in the phrases relating to capital and extreme punishment.<br />2) metaphorically anything supreme, chief, prominent<br />2a) of persons, master lord: of a husband in relation to his wife<br />2b) of Christ: the Lord of the husband and of the Church<br />2c) of things: the corner stone<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:673, 429|<b>G2776</b><br />Probably from the primary word<span class="uni">κάπτω</span> kapt&ocirc; (in the sense of <i>seizing</i>); the <i>head</i> (as the part most readily <i>taken</i> hold of), literally or figuratively: - head.<br />|2776|
κεφαλίς|kefal�j|kevalis|1) a little head<br />2) the highest part, extremity of anything<br />2a) as the capital of a column<br />2b) the tips or knobs of the wooden rod around which parchments were rolled were called by this word, because they resembled little heads<br />3) the Alexandrian writers transferred the name to the roll or volume itself<br />3a) in the roll of the book<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine|<b>G2777</b><br />From G2776; properly a <i>knob</i>, that is, (by implication) a <i>roll</i> (by extension from the <i>end</i> of a stick on which the <i>manuscript</i> was rolled): - volume.<br />|2777|
κῆνσος|k�nsoj|kgnsps|1) census (among the Romans, denoting a register and valuation of property in accordance with which taxes were paid), in the NT the tax or tribute levied on individuals and to be paid yearly. (our capitation or poll tax)<br />2) the coin with which the tax is paid, tribute money<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine|<b>G2778</b><br />Of Latin origin; properly an <i>enrolment</i> ("census"), that is, (by implication) a <i>tax:</i> - tribute.<br />|2778|
κῆπος|k�poj|kgqps|1) a garden<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine|<b>G2779</b><br />Of uncertain affinity; a <i>garden:</i> - garden.<br />|2779|
κηπουρός|khpour�j|kgqpurps|1) a keeper of a garden, a gardener<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine|<b>G2780</b><br />From G2779 and <span class="uni">οὖρος</span> ouros (a <i>warden</i>); a <i>garden keeper</i>, that is, <i>gardener:</i> - gardener.<br />|2780|
κηριον|khrion|kgripn|1) honeycomb<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter|<b>G2781</b><br />Diminutive from <span class="uni">κηός</span> k&ecirc;os (<i>wax</i>); a <i>cell</i> for honey, that is, (collectively) the <i>comb:</i> - [honey-] comb.<br />|2781|
κήρυγμα|k�rugma|kgrucma|1) that which is proclaimed by a herald or public crier, a proclamation by herald<br />2) in the NT the message or proclamation of the heralds of God or Christ<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:714, 430|<b>G2782</b><br />From G2784; a <i>proclamation</i> (especially of the gospel; by implication the <i>gospel</i> itself): - preaching.<br />|2782|
κῆρυξ|k�rux|kgruo|1) a herald or messenger vested with public authority, who conveyed the official messages of kings, magistrates, princes, military commanders, or who gave a public summons or demand, and performed various other duties. In the NT God's ambassador, and the herald or proclaimer of the divine word.<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:683, 430|<b>G2783</b><br />From G2784; a <i>herald</i>, that is, of divine truth (especially of the gospel): - preacher.<br />|2783|
κηρύσσω|khr�ssw|kgrussz|1) to be a herald, to officiate as a herald<br />1a) to proclaim after the manner of a herald<br />1b) always with the suggestion of formality, gravity and an authority which must be listened to and obeyed<br />2) to publish, proclaim openly: something which has been done<br />3) used of the public proclamation of the gospel and matters pertaining to it, made by John the Baptist, by Jesus, by the apostles and other Christian teachers<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:697, 430|<b>G2784</b><br />Of uncertain affinity; to <i>herald</i> (as a public <i>crier</i>), especially divine truth (the gospel): - preach (-er), proclaim, publish.<br />|2784|
κῆτος|k�toj|kgtps|1) a sea monster, whale, huge fish<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter|<b>G2785</b><br />Probably from the base of G5490; a huge <i>fish</i> (as <i>gaping</i> for prey): - whale.<br />|2785|
Κηφᾶς|Khf�j|kgvas|Cephas = "stone"<br />1) another name for the apostle Peter<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun proper masculine|<b>G2786</b><br />Of Chaldee origin (compare [H3710]); <i>the Rock</i>; <i>Cephas</i> (that is, <i>Kepha</i>), surname of Peter: - Cephas.<br />|2786|
κιβωτός|kibwt�j|kibztps|1) a wooden chest or box<br />2) in the NT the ark of the covenant, in the temple at Jerusalem<br />3) of Noah's vessel built in the form of an ark<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine|<b>G2787</b><br />Of uncertain derivative; a <i>box</i>, that is, the sacred <i>ark</i> and that of Noah: - ark.<br />|2787|
κιθάρα|kiq�ra|kihara|1) a harp to which praises of God are sung in heaven<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine|<b>G2788</b><br />Of uncertain affinity; a <i>lyre:</i> - harp.<br />|2788|
κιθαρίζω|kiqar�zw|kiharifz|1) to play upon the harp<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb|<b>G2789</b><br />From G2788; to <i>play on a lyre:</i> - harp.<br />|2789|
κιθαρῳδός|kiqarJd�j|kiharzdps|1) a harper, one who plays the harp and accompanies it with his voice<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine|<b>G2790</b><br />From G2788 and a derivative of the same as G5603; a <i>lyre singer</i> (<i>player</i>), that is, <i>harpist:</i> - harper.<br />|2790|
Κιλικία|Kilik�a|kilikia|Cilicia = "the land of Celix"<br />1) a maritime province in the southeast of Asia Minor, boarding on Pamphylia in the west, Lycaonia and Cappadocia in the north and Syria in the east. Its capital, Tarsus, was the birth place of Paul<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun proper locative|<b>G2791</b><br />Probably of foreign origin; <i>Cilicia</i>, a region of Asia Minor: - Cilicia.<br />|2791|
κιννάμωμον|kinn�mwmon|kinnamzmpn|1) cinnamon was a well known aromatic substance, the rind of "Laurus cinnamonum" called "Korunda-gauhah" in Ceylon<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter|<b>G2792</b><br />Of foreign origin (compare [H7076]); <i>cinnamon:</i> - cinnamon.<br />|2792|
κινδυνεύω|kindune�w|kinduneuz|1) to be in jeopardy, to be in danger, to put in peril<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb|<b>G2793</b><br />From G2794; to <i>undergo peril:</i> - be in danger, be (stand) in jeopardy.<br />|2793|
