κλάδος|kl�doj|kladps|1) a young tender shoot, broken off for grafting<br />2) a branch<br />2a) as the Jewish patriarchs are likened to a root, so their posterity are likened to branches<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:720,*|<b>G2798</b><br />From G2806; a <i>twig</i> or <i>bough</i> (as if broken off): - branch.<br />|2798|
κλαίω|kla�w|klaiz|1) to mourn, weep, lament<br />1a) weeping as the sign of pain and grief for the thing signified (i.e. for the pain and grief)<br />1b) of those who mourn for the dead<br />2) to weep for, mourn for, bewail, one<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:722, 436|<b>G2799</b><br />Of uncertain affinity; to <i>sob</i>, that is, <i>wail</i> aloud (whereas G1145 is rather to <i>cry</i> silently): - bewail. weep.<br />|2799|
κλάσις|kl�sij|klasis|1) a breaking<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:726, 437|<b>G2800</b><br />From G2806; <i>fracture</i> (the act): - breaking.<br />|2800|
κλάσμα|kl�sma|klasma|1) a fragment, broken piece<br />1a) remnants of food<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:726, 437|<b>G2801</b><br />From G2806; a <i>piece</i> (<i>bit</i>): - broken, fragment.<br />|2801|
Καῦδα|Ka�da|kauda|Clauda = "lame"<br />1) a small island nearly due west of Cape Malta on the south coast<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun proper locative|<b>G2802</b><br />Of uncertain derivation; <i>Claude</i>, an island near Crete: - Clauda.<br />|2802|
Κλαυδία|Klaud�a|klaudia|Claudia = "lame"<br />1) a Christian woman<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun proper feminine|<b>G2803</b><br />Feminine of G2804; <i>Claudia</i>, a Christian woman: - Claudia.<br />|2803|
Κλαύδιος|Kla�dioj|klaudips|Claudius = "lame"<br />1) C. Caesar the name of the fourth Roman emperor, who came to power in 41 A.D. and was poisoned by his wife Agrippina, in 54 A.D.<br />2) C. Lysias a tribune of the Roman cohort who rescued Paul from the hands of the mob at Jerusalem<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun proper masculine|<b>G2804</b><br />Of Latin origin; <i>Claudius</i>, the name of two Romans: - Claudius.<br />|2804|
κλαυθμός|klauqm�j|klauhmps|1) weeping, lamentation<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:725, 436|<b>G2805</b><br />From G2799; <i>lamentation:</i> - wailing, weeping, X wept.<br />|2805|
κλάω|kl�w|klaz|1) to break<br />1a) used in the NT of the breaking of bread or communion<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:726, 437|<b>G2806</b><br />A primary verb; to <i>break</i> (specifically of bread): - break.<br />|2806|
κλείς|kle�j|kleis|1) a key<br />1a) since the keeper of the keys has the power to open and to shut<br />1b) metaphorically in the NT to denote power and authority of various kinds<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:744, 439|<b>G2807</b><br />From G2808; a <i>key</i> (as <i>shutting</i> a lock), literally or figuratively: - key.<br />|2807|
κλείω|kle�w|kleiz|1) to shut, shut up<br />2) metaphorically<br />2a) to cause the heavens to withhold rain<br />2b) to shut up compassion so that it is like a thing inaccessible to one, to be devoid of pity towards one<br />2c) to obstruct the entrance into the kingdom of heaven<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb|<b>G2808</b><br />A primary verb; to <i>close</i> (literally or figuratively): - shut (up).<br />|2808|
κλέμμα|kl�mma|klemma|1) thing stolen<br />2) the act of theft<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter|<b>G2809</b><br />From G2813; <i>stealing</i> (properly the thing stolen, but used of the act): - theft.<br />|2809|
Κλεοπᾶς|Kleop�j|klepqas|Cleopas = "of a renowned father"<br />1) one of the two disciples who were going to Emmaus on the day of the resurrection<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun proper masculine|<b>G2810</b><br />Probably contracted from <span class="uni">Κλεόπατρος</span> Kleopatros (compounded from G2811 and G3962); <i>Cleopas</i>, a Christian: - Cleopas.<br />|2810|
κλέος|kl�oj|kleps|1) rumour, report<br />2) glory, praise<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter|<b>G2811</b><br />From a shorter form of G2564; <i>renown</i> (as if <i>being called</i>): - glory.<br />|2811|
κλέπτης|kl�pthj|kleqtgs|1) an embezzler, pilferer<br />1a) the name is transferred to false teachers, who do not care to instruct men, but abuse their confidence for their own gain<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:754, 441|<b>G2812</b><br />From G2813; a <i>stealer</i> (literally or figuratively): - thief. Compare G3027.<br />|2812|
κλέπτω|kl�ptw|kleqtz|1) to steal<br />1a) to commit a theft<br />1b) take away by theft i.e take away by stealth<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:754, 441|<b>G2813</b><br />A primary verb; to <i>filch:</i> - steal.<br />|2813|
κλῆμα|kl�ma|klgma|1) a tender and flexible branch<br />2) spec. the shoot or branch of a vine, a vine sprout<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:757, 441|<b>G2814</b><br />From G2806; a <i>limb</i> or <i>shoot</i> (as if <i>broken</i> off): - branch.<br />|2814|
Κλῆμης|Kl�mhj|klgmgs|Clement = "mild, merciful"<br />1) a companion of Paul and apparently a member of the church of Philippi. According to tradition, he is identical with that Clement who was bishop of Rome towards the close of the first century.<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun proper masculine|<b>G2815</b><br />Of Latin origin; <i>merciful</i>; <i>Clemes</i> (that is, <i>Clemens</i>), a Christian: - Clement.<br />|2815|
κληρονομέω|klhronom�w|klgrpnpmez|1) to receive a lot, receive by lot<br />1a) especially to receive a part of an inheritance, receive as an inheritance, obtain by right of inheritance<br />1b) to be an heir, to inherit<br />2) to receive the portion assigned to one, receive an allotted portion, receive as one's own or as a possession<br />3) to become partaker of, to obtain<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:767, 442|<b>G2816</b><br />From G2818; to <i>be</i> an <i>heir</i> to (literally or figuratively): - be heir, (obtain by) inherit (-ance).<br />|2816|
κληρονομία|klhronom�a|klgrpnpmia|1) an inheritance, property received (or to be received) by inheritance<br />2) what is given to one as a possession<br />2a) the eternal blessedness of the consummated kingdom of God which is to be expected after the visible return of Christ<br />2b) the share which an individual will have in that eternal blessedness<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:767, 442|<b>G2817</b><br />From G2818; <i>heirship</i>, that is, (concretely) a <i>patrimony</i> or (generally) a <i>possession:</i> - inheritance.<br />|2817|
κληρονόμος|klhron�moj|klgrpnpmps|1) one who receives by lot, an heir<br />1a) an heir<br />1b) in Messianic usage, one who receives his allotted possession by right of sonship<br />2) one who has acquired or obtained the portion allotted to him<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:767, 442|<b>G2818</b><br />From G2819 and the base of G3551 (in its original sense of <i>partitioning</i>, that is, [reflexively] <i>getting</i> by apportionment); a <i>sharer by lot</i>, that is, an <i>inheritor</i> (literally or figuratively); by implication a <i>possessor:</i> - heir.<br />|2818|
κλῆρος|kl�roj|klgrps|1) an object used in casting or drawing lots, which was either a pebble, or a potsherd, or a bit of wood<br />1a) the lots of several persons concerned, inscribed with their names, were thrown together into a vase, which was then shaken, and he whose lot fell out first upon the ground was the one chosen<br />2) what is obtained by lot, allotted portion<br />2a) a portion of the ministry common to the apostles<br />2b) used of the part which one will have in eternal salvation<br />2b1) of salvation itself<br />2b2) the eternal salvation which God has assigned to the saints<br />2c) of persons<br />2c1) those whose care and oversight has been assigned to one [allotted charge], used of Christian churches, the administration of which falls to the lot of presbyters<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:758, 442|<b>G2819</b><br />Probably from G2806 (through the idea of using <i>bits</i> of wood, etc., for the purpose); a <i>die</i> (for drawing chances); by implication a <i>portion</i> (as if so secured); by extension an <i>acquisition</i> (especially a <i>patrimony</i>, figuratively): - heritage, inheritance, lot, part.<br />|2819|
κληρόω|klhr�w|klgrpz|1) to cast lots, determine by lot<br />2) to choose by lot<br />3) to allot, assign by lot<br />3a) on to another as a possession<br />4) in NT: to make a lot, i.e. a heritage, private possession<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:764, 442|<b>G2820</b><br />From G2819; to <i>allot</i>, that is, (figuratively) to <i>assign</i> (a privilege): - obtain an inheritance.<br />|2820|
κλῆσις|kl�sij|klgsis|1) a calling, calling to<br />2) a call, invitation<br />2a) to a feast<br />2b) of the divine invitation to embrace salvation of God<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:491, 394|<b>G2821</b><br />From a shorter form of G2564; an <i>invitation</i> (figuratively): - calling.<br />|2821|
κλητός|klht�j|klgtps|1) called, invited (to a banquet)<br />1a) invited (by God in the proclamation of the Gospel) to obtain eternal salvation in the kingdom through Christ<br />1b) called to (the discharge of) some office<br />1b1) divinely selected and appointed<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adjective<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:494, 394|<b>G2822</b><br />From the same as G2821; <i>invited</i>, that is, <i>appointed</i>, or (specifically) a <i>saint:</i> - called.<br />|2822|
κλίβανος|kl�banoj|klibanps|1) a clibanus, an earthen vessel for baking bread. It was broader at the bottom than above at the orifice, and when sufficiently heated by a fire kindled within, the dough was baked by being spread upon the outside. [But according to others, the dough was baked by being placed inside and the fire or coals outside, the vessel being perforated with small holes that the heat might better penetrate.]<br />2) a furnace, oven<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine|<b>G2823</b><br />Of uncertain derivation; an earthen <i>pot</i> used for baking in: - oven.<br />|2823|
κλίμα|kl�ma|klima|1) an inclination, slope, declivity<br />2) the [supposed] sloping of the earth from the equator towards the poles, a zone<br />3) a tract of land, a region<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter|<b>G2824</b><br />From G2827; a <i>slope</i>, that is, (specifically) a "clime" or <i>tract</i> of country: - part, region.<br />|2824|
κλίνη|kl�nh|kling|1) a small bed, a couch<br />2) a couch to recline on at meals<br />3) a couch on which a sick man is carried<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter|<b>G2825</b><br />From G2827; a <i>couch</i> (for sleep, sickness, sitting or eating): - bed, table.<br />|2825|
