Κυρήνη|Kur�nh|kurgng|Cyrene = "supremacy of the bridle"<br />1) a large and very flourishing city of Libya Cyrenaica or Pentapolitana, about 11 miles (17 km) from the sea. Among its inhabitants were a great number of Jews, whom Ptolemy I. had brought there, and invested with the right of citizens<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun proper locative|<b>G2957</b><br />Of uncertain derivation; <i>Cyrene</i>, a region of Africa: - Cyrene.<br />|2957|
Κυρήνιος|Kur�nioj|kurgnips|Cyrenius = "warrior"<br />1) the Greek form of the Roman name Quirinus. His full name is Publius Sulpicius Quirinus. He was consul B.C. 12., and was made governor of Syria after the banishment of Archelaus in A.D. 6. He was probably twice governor of Syria; his first governorship extended from B.C. 4 (the year of Christ's birth) to B.C. 1. It was during this time that he was sent to make enrolment which caused Joseph and Mary to visit Bethlehem. Luk 2:2. The second enrolment is mentioned in Act 5:37.<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun proper masculine|<b>G2958</b><br />Of Latin origin; <i>Cyrenius</i> (that is, <i>Quirinus</i>), a Roman: - Cyrenius.<br />|2958|
