νόμος|n�moj|npmps|1) anything established, anything received by usage, a custom, a law, a command<br />1a) of any law whatsoever<br />1a1) a law or rule producing a state approved of God<br />1a1a) by the observance of which is approved of God<br />1a2) a precept or injunction<br />1a3) the rule of action prescribed by reason<br />1b) of the Mosaic law, and referring, acc. to the context. either to the volume of the law or to its contents<br />1c) the Christian religion: the law demanding faith, the moral instruction given by Christ, especially the precept concerning love<br />1d) the name of the more important part (the Pentateuch), is put for the entire collection of the sacred books of the OT<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 4:1022, 646|<b>G3551</b><br />From a primary word <span class="uni">νέμω</span> nem&ocirc; (to <i>parcel</i> out, especially <i>food</i> or <i>grazing</i> to animals); <i>law</i> (through the idea of prescriptive <i>usage</i>), generally (<i>regulation</i>), specifically (of Moses [including the volume]; also of the Gospel), or figuratively (a <i>principle</i>): - law.<br />|3551|
νοσέω|nos�w|npsez|1) to be sick<br />2) metaphorically of any ailment of the mind<br />2a) to be taken with such an interest in a thing as amounts to a disease, to have a morbid fondness for<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 4:1091, 655|<b>G3552</b><br />From G3554; to <i>be sick</i>, that is, (by implication of a diseased appetite) to <i>hanker</i> after (figuratively to <i>harp</i> upon): - dote.<br />|3552|
