οὐκέτι|o�k�ti|puketi|1) no longer, no more, no further<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adverb|<b>G3765</b><br />Also (separately) <span class="uni">οὐκ ἔτι</span> ouk eti <i>ook et'-ee</i><br />>From G3756 and G2089; <i>not yet</i>, <i>no longer:</i> - after that (not), (not) any more, henceforth (hereafter), not, no longer (more), not as yet (now), now no more (not), yet (not).<br />|3765|
οὐκοῦν|o�ko�n|pukpun|1) not therefore<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adverb|<b>G3766</b><br />From G3756 and G3767; is it <i>not therefore</i> that, that is, (affirmatively) <i>hence</i> or <i>so:</i> - then.<br />|3766|
οὖν|o�n|pun|1) then, therefore, accordingly, consequently, these things being so<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> particle|<b>G3767</b><br />Apparently a primary word; (adverbially) <i>certainly</i>, or (conjugationally) <i>accordingly:</i> - and (so, truly), but, now (then), so (likewise then), then, therefore, verily, wherefore.<br />|3767|
οὔπω|o�pw|puqz|1) not yet<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adverb|<b>G3768</b><br />From G3756 and G4452; <i>not yet:</i> - hitherto not, (no . . .) as yet, not yet.<br />|3768|
οὐρά|o�r�|pura|1) a tail<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine|<b>G3769</b><br />Apparently a primary word; a <i>tail:</i> - tail.<br />|3769|
οὐράνιος|o�r�nioj|puranips|1) heavenly<br />1a) dwelling in heaven<br />1b) coming from heaven<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adjective<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:536, 736|<b>G3770</b><br />From G3772; <i>celestial</i>, that is, <i>belonging to</i> or <i>coming from</i> the <i>sky:</i> - heavenly.<br />|3770|
οὐρανόθεν|o�ran�qen|puranphen|1) from heaven<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adverb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:542, 736|<b>G3771</b><br />From G3772 and the enclitic of source; <i>from</i> the <i>sky:</i> - from heaven.<br />|3771|
οὐρανός|o�ran�j|puranps|1) the vaulted expanse of the sky with all things visible in it<br />1a) the universe, the world<br />1b) the aerial heavens or sky, the region where the clouds and the tempests gather, and where thunder and lightning are produced<br />1c) the sidereal or starry heavens<br />2) the region above the sidereal heavens, the seat of order of things eternal and consummately perfect where God dwells and other heavenly beings<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:497, 736|<b>G3772</b><br />Perhaps from the same as G3735 (through the idea of <i>elevation</i>); the <i>sky</i>; by extension <i>heaven</i> (as the abode of God); by implication <i>happiness</i>, <i>power</i>, <i>eternity</i>; specifically the <i>Gospel</i> (<i>Christianity</i>): - air, heaven ([-ly]), sky.<br />|3772|
Οὐρβανός|O�rban�j|purbanps|Urbane = "of the city; polite"<br />1) a certain Christian in Rom 16:9<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun proper masculine|<b>G3773</b><br />Of Latin origin; <i>Urbanus</i> (<i>of</i> the <i>city</i>, "urbane"), a Christian: - Urbanus.<br />|3773|
Οὐρίας|O�r�aj|purias|Uriah = "light of Jehovah"<br />1) the husband of Bathsheba, one of David's mighty men<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun proper masculine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 3:1,*|<b>G3774</b><br />Of Hebrew origin [H223]; <i>Urias</i> (that is, <i>Urijah</i>), a Hittite: - Urias.<br />|3774|
οὖς|o�j|pus|1) the ear<br />2) metaphorically the faculty of perceiving with the mind, the faculty of understanding and knowing<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:543, 744|<b>G3775</b><br />Apparently a primary word; the <i>ear</i> (physically or mentally): - ear.<br />|3775|
οὐσία|o�s�a|pusia|1) what one has, i.e. property, possessions, estate<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine|<b>G3776</b><br />From the feminine of G5607; <i>substance</i>, that is, <i>property</i> (<i>possessions</i>): - goods, substance.<br />|3776|
οὔτε|o�te|pute|1) neither, and not<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adverb|<b>G3777</b><br />From G3756 and G5037; <i>not too</i>, that is, <i>neither</i> or <i>nor</i>; by analogy <i>not even:</i> - neither, none, nor (yet), (no, yet) not, nothing.<br />|3777|
οὗτος|o�toj|putps|1) this, these, etc.<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> pronoun|<b>G3778</b><br />Including the nominative masculine plural (second form), nominative feminine signular (third form), and the nominate feminine plural, (fourth form). From the article G3588 and G846; <i>the he</i> (<i>she</i> or it), that is, <i>this</i> or <i>that</i> (often with the article repeated): - he (it was that), hereof, it, she, such as, the same, these, they, this (man, same, woman), which, who.<br />|3778|
οὕτω(ς)|o�twj|putzs|1) in this manner, thus, so<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adverb|<b>G3779</b><br />Or, before a vowel, <span class="uni">οὕτως</span> hout&ocirc;s <i>hoo'-toce</i>.<br />From G3778; <i>in this way</i> (referring to what precedes or follows): - after that, after (in) this manner, as, even (so), for all that, like (-wise), no more, on this fashion (-wise), so (in like manner), thus, what.<br />|3779|
ὀφειλέτης|�feil�thj|pveiletgs|1) one who owes another, a debtor<br />1a) one held by some obligation, bound by some duty<br />1b) one who has not yet made amends to whom he has injured:<br />1b1) one who owes God penalty or whom God can demand punishment as something due, i.e. a sinner<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:565, 746|<b>G3781</b><br />From G3784; an <i>ower</i>, that is, a person <i>indebted</i>; figuratively a <i>delinquent</i>; morally a <i>transgressor</i> (against God): - debtor, which owed, sinner.<br />|3781|
ὀφειλή|�feil�|pveilg|1) that which is owed<br />2) a debt<br />3) metaphorically dues: specifically of conjugal duty<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:564, 746|<b>G3782</b><br />From G3784; <i>indebtedness</i>, that is, (concretely) a <i>sum</i> owed; figuratively <i>obligation</i>, that is, (conjugal) <i>duty:</i> - debt, due.<br />|3782|
ὀφείλημα|�fe�lhma|pveilgma|1) that which is owed<br />1a) that which is justly or legally due, a debt<br />2) metaphorically offence, sin<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:565, 746|<b>G3783</b><br />From (the alternate of) G3784; <i>something owed</i>, that is, (figuratively) a <i>due</i>.; morally a <i>fault:</i> - debt.<br />|3783|
ὀφείλω|�fe�lw|pveilz|1) to owe<br />1a) to owe money, be in debt for<br />1a1) that which is due, the debt<br />2) metaphorically the goodwill due<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:559, 746|<b>G3784</b><br />Including its prolonged form (second form) used in certain tenses. Probably from the base of G3786 (through the idea of <i>accruing</i>); to <i>owe</i> (pecuniarily); figuratively to <i>be under obligation</i> (<i>ought</i>, <i>must</i>, <i>should</i>); morally to <i>fail</i> in duty: - behove, be bound, (be) debt (-or), (be) due (-ty), be guilty (indebted), (must) need (-s), ought, owe, should. See also G3785.<br />|3784|
ὄφελον|�felon|pvelpn|1) would that, where one wishes that a thing had happened which has not happened or a thing be done which probably will not be done<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> particle|<b>G3785</b><br />First person singular of a past tense of G3784; I <i>ought</i> (<i>wish</i>), that is, (interjectionally) <i>oh that!</i>: - would (to God).<br />|3785|
ὄφελος|�feloj|pvelps|1) advantage, profit<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter|<b>G3786</b><br />From <span class="uni">ὀφέλλω</span> ophell&ocirc; (to <i>heap</i> up, that is, <i>accumulate</i> or <i>benefit</i>); <i>gain:</i> - advantageth, profit.<br />|3786|
ὀφθαλμοδουλία|�fqalmodoul�a|pvhalmpdpulia|1) service performed [only] under the master's eyes<br />1a) for the master's eye usually stimulates to greater diligence<br />1b) his absence, on the other hand, renders sluggish<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 2:280, 182|<b>G3787</b><br />From G3788 and G1397; <i>sight labor</i>, that is, that needs watching (<i>remissness</i>): - eye-service.<br />|3787|
ὀφθαλμός|�fqalm�j|pvhalmps|1) the eye<br />2) metaphorically the eyes of the mind, the faculty of knowing<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:375, 706|<b>G3788</b><br />From G3700; the <i>eye</i> (literally or figuratively); by implication <i>vision</i>; figuratively <i>envy</i> (from the jealous side glance): - eye, sight.<br />|3788|
ὄφις|�fij|pvis|1) snake, serpent<br />2) with the ancients, the serpent was an emblem of cunning and wisdom. The serpent who deceived Eve was regarded by the Jews as the devil.<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:566, 748|<b>G3789</b><br />Probably from G3700 (through the idea of <i>sharpness</i> of vision); a <i>snake</i>, figuratively (as a type of sly cunning) an artful <i>malicious</i> person, especially <i>Satan:</i> - serpent.<br />|3789|
ὀφρῦς|�fr�j|pvrus|1) the eyebrow<br />2) any prominence or projection<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine|<b>G3790</b><br />Perhaps from G3700 (through the idea of the shading or proximity to the organ of <i>vision</i>); the eye "brow" or <i>forehead</i>, that is, (figuratively) the <i>brink</i> of a precipice: - brow.<br />|3790|
ὀχλέομαι|�cl�omai|pxlepmai|1) to excite a mob against one<br />2) to disturb, roll away<br />3) to trouble, molest<br />4) be in confusion, in an uproar<br />5) to be vexed, molested, troubled: by demons<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb|<b>G3791</b><br />From G3793; to <i>mob</i>, that is, (by implication) to <i>harass:</i> - vex.<br />|3791|
ὀχλοποιέω|�clopoi�w|pxlpqpiez|1) to collect a crowd, gather the people together<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb|<b>G3792</b><br />From G3793 and G4160; to <i>make a crowd</i>, that is, <i>raise a</i> public <i>disturbance:</i> - gather a company.<br />|3792|
ὄχλος|�cloj|pxlps|1) a crowd<br />1a) a casual collection of people<br />1a1) a multitude of men who have flocked together in some place<br />1a2) a throng<br />1b) a multitude<br />1b1) the common people, as opposed to the rulers and leading men<br />1b2) with contempt: the ignorant multitude, the populace<br />1c) a multitude<br />1c1) the multitudes, seems to denote troops of people gathered together without order<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:582, 750|<b>G3793</b><br />From a derivative of G2192 (meaning a <i>vehicle</i>); a <i>throng</i> (as <i>borne</i> along); by implication the <i>rabble</i>; by extension a <i>class</i> of people; figuratively a <i>riot:</i> - company, multitude, number (of people), people, press.<br />|3793|
ὀχύρωμα|�c�rwma|pxurzma|1) a castle, stronghold, fortress, fastness<br />2) anything on which one relies<br />2a) of the arguments and reasonings by which a disputant endeavours to fortify his opinion and defend it against his opponent<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:590, 752|<b>G3794</b><br />From a remote derivative of G2192 (meaning to <i>fortify</i>, through the idea of <i>holding</i> safely); a <i>castle</i> (figuratively <i>argument</i>): - stronghold.<br />|3794|
ὀψάριον|�y�rion|pyaripn|1) fish<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter|<b>G3795</b><br />Neuter of a presumed derivative of the base of G3702; a <i>relish</i> to other food (as if cooked <i>sauce</i>), that is, (specifically) <i>fish</i> (presumably salted and dried as a condiment): - fish.<br />|3795|
ὀψέ|�y�|pye|1) after a long time, long after, late<br />1a) late in the day, i.e. at evening<br />1b) the sabbath having just passed, after the sabbath<br />1b1) at the early dawn of the first day of the week<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb|<b>G3796</b><br />From the same as G3694 (through the idea of <i>backwardness</i>); (adverbially) <i>late</i> in the day; by extension <i>after the close</i> of the day: - (at) even, in the end.<br />|3796|
ὄψιμος|�yimoj|pyimps|1) late, later<br />1a) of the time of subsidence of the waters of the Nile<br />1b) the latter or vernal rain, which falls chiefly in the months of March and April just before the harvest<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adjective|<b>G3797</b><br />From G3796; <i>later</i>, that is, <i>vernal</i> (showering): - latter.<br />|3797|
ὀψία|�y�a|pyia|1) late<br />2) evening<br />2a) either from three to six o'clock p.m.<br />2b) from six o'clock p.m. to the beginning of night<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adjective|<b>G3798</b><br />From G3796; <i>late</i>; feminine (as noun) <i>afternoon</i> (early eve) or <i>nightfall</i> (later eve): - even (-ing, [-tide]).<br />|3798|
ὄψις|�yij|pyis|1) seeing, sight<br />2) face, countenance<br />3) the outward appearance, look<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine|<b>G3799</b><br />From G3700; properly <i>sight</i> (the act), that is, (by implication) the <i>visage</i>, an external <i>show:</i> - appearance, countenance, face.<br />|3799|
ὀψώνιον|�y�nion|pyznipn|1) a soldier's pay, allowance<br />1a) that part of the soldier's support given in place of pay [i.e. rations] and the money in which he is paid<br />2) metaphorically wages: hire or pay of sin<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:591, 752|<b>G3800</b><br />Neuter of a presumed derivative of the same as G3795; <i>rations</i> for a soldier, that is, (by extension) his <i>stipend</i> or <i>pay:</i> - wages.<br />|3800|
παγιδεύω|pagide�w|qacideuz|1) to ensnare, entrap<br />1a) of birds<br />2) metaphorically of the attempt to elicit from one some remark which can be turned into an accusation against him<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:595, 752|<b>G3802</b><br />From G3803; to <i>ensnare</i> (figuratively): - entangle.<br />|3802|
παγίς|pag�j|qacis|1) snare, trap, noose<br />1a) of snares in which birds are entangled and caught<br />1a1) implies unexpectedly, suddenly, because birds and beasts are caught unawares<br />1b) a snare, i.e. whatever brings peril, loss, destruction<br />1b1) of a sudden and unexpected deadly peril<br />1b2) of the allurements and seductions of sin<br />1b3) the allurements to sin by which the devil holds one bound<br />1b4) the snares of love<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:593, 752|<b>G3803</b><br />From G4078; a <i>trap</i> (as <i>fastened</i> by a noose or notch); figuratively a <i>trick</i> or <i>stratagem</i> (<i>temptation</i>): - snare<br />|3803|
πάθημα|p�qhma|qahgma|1) that which one suffers or has suffered<br />1a) externally, a suffering, misfortune, calamity, evil, affliction<br />1a1) of the sufferings of Christ<br />1a2) also the afflictions which Christians must undergo in behalf of the same cause which Christ patiently endured<br />1b) of an inward state, an affliction, passion<br />2) an enduring, undergoing, suffering<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:930, 798|<b>G3804</b><br />From a presumed derivative of G3806; something <i>undergone</i>, that is, <i>hardship</i> or <i>pain</i>; subjectively an <i>emotion</i> or <i>influence:</i> - affection, affliction, motion, suffering.<br />|3804|
παθητός|paqht�j|qahgtps|1) passible, endued with the capacity of suffering, capable of feeling<br />2) subject to the necessity of suffering, destined to suffer<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adjective<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:924, 798|<b>G3805</b><br />From the same as G3804; <i>liable</i> (that is, <i>doomed</i>) <i>to</i> experience <i>pain:</i> - suffer.<br />|3805|
πάθος|p�qoj|qahps|1) whatever befalls one, whether it be sad or joyous<br />1a) spec. a calamity, mishap, evil, affliction<br />2) a feeling which the mind suffers<br />2a) an affliction of the mind, emotion, passion<br />2b) passionate deed<br />2c) used by the Greeks in either a good or bad sense<br />2d) in the NT in a bad sense, depraved passion, vile passions<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:926, 798|<b>G3806</b><br />From the alternate of G3958; properly <i>suffering</i> ("pathos"), that is, (subjectively) a <i>passion</i> (especially <i>concupiscence</i>): - (inordinate) affection, lust.<br />|3806|
παιδαγωγός|paidagwg�j|qaidaczcps|1) a tutor, i.e. a guardian and guide of boys. Among the Greeks and the Romans the name was applied to trustworthy slaves who were charged with the duty of supervising the life and morals of boys belonging to the better class. The boys were not allowed so much as to step out of the house without them before arriving at the age of manhood.<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:596, 753|<b>G3807</b><br />From G3816 and a reduplication form of G71; a <i>boy leader</i>, that is, a servant whose office it was to take the children to school; (by implication [figuratively] a <i>tutor</i> ["paedagogue"]): - instructor, schoolmaster.<br />|3807|
παιδάριον|paid�rion|qaidaripn|1) little boy, a lad<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:636, 759|<b>G3808</b><br />Neuter of a presumed derivative of G3816; a <i>little boy:</i> - child, lad.<br />|3808|
παιδεία|paide�a|qaideia|1) the whole training and education of children (which relates to the cultivation of mind and morals, and employs for this purpose now commands and admonitions, now reproof and punishment) It also includes the training and care of the body<br />2) whatever in adults also cultivates the soul, especially by correcting mistakes and curbing passions.<br />2a) instruction which aims at increasing virtue<br />2b) chastisement, chastening, (of the evils with which God visits men for their amendment)<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:596, 753|<b>G3809</b><br />From G3811; <i>tutorage</i>, that is, <i>education</i> or <i>training</i>; by implication disciplinary <i>correction:</i> - chastening, chastisement, instruction, nurture.<br />|3809|
παιδευτής|paideut�j|qaideutgs|1) an instructor, preceptor, teacher<br />2) a chastiser<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:596, 753|<b>G3810</b><br />From G3811; a <i>trainer</i>, that is, <i>teacher</i> or (by implication) <i>discipliner:</i> - which corrected, instructor.<br />|3810|
παιδεύω|paide�w|qaideuz|1) to train children<br />1a) to be instructed or taught or learn<br />1b) to cause one to learn<br />2) to chastise<br />2a) to chastise or castigate with words, to correct<br />2a1) of those who are moulding the character of others by reproof and admonition<br />2b) of God<br />2b1) to chasten by the affliction of evils and calamities<br />2c) to chastise with blows, to scourge<br />2c1) of a father punishing his son<br />2c2) of a judge ordering one to be scourged<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:596, 753|<b>G3811</b><br />From G3816; to <i>train</i> up a child, that is, <i>educate</i>, or (by implication) <i>discipline</i> (by punishment): - chasten (-ise), instruct, learn, teach.<br />|3811|
παιδιόθεν|paidi�qen|qaidiphen|1) from childhood, from a child<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adverb|<b>G3812</b><br />Adverb (of <i>source</i>) from G3813; <i>from infancy:</i> - of a child.<br />|3812|
παιδίον|paid�on|qaidipn|1) a young child, a little boy, a little girl<br />1a) infants<br />1b) children, little ones<br />1c) an infant<br />1c1) of a (male) child just recently born<br />1d) of a more advanced child; of a mature child<br />1e) metaphorically children (like children) in intellect<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:636, 759|<b>G3813</b><br />Neuter diminutive of G3816; a <i>childling</i> (of either sex), that is, (properly) an infant, or (by extension) a half grown <i>boy</i> or girl; figuratively an <i>immature</i> Christian: - (little, young) child, damsel.<br />|3813|
παιδίσκη|paid�skh|qaidiskg|1) a young girl, damsel<br />2) a maid-servant, a young female slave<br />2a) a maid servant who has charge of the door<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine|<b>G3814</b><br />Feminine diminutive of G3816; a <i>girl</i>, that is, (specifically) a <i>female slave</i> or <i>servant:</i> - bondmaid (-woman), damsel, maid (-en).<br />|3814|
παίζω|pa�zw|qaifz|1) to play like a child<br />2) to play, sport, jest<br />3) to give way to hilarity, especially by joking singing, dancing<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:625, 758|<b>G3815</b><br />From G3816; to <i>sport</i> (as a boy): - play.<br />|3815|
παῖς|pa�j|qais|1) a child, boy or girl<br />1a) infants, children<br />2) servant, slave<br />2a) an attendant, servant, spec. a king's attendant, minister<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine or feminine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:636, 759|<b>G3816</b><br />Perhaps from G3817; a <i>boy</i> (as often <i>beaten</i> with impunity), or (by analogy) a <i>girl</i>, and (generally) a <i>child</i>; specifically a <i>slave</i> or <i>servant</i> (especially a <i>minister</i> to a king; and by eminence to God): - child, maid (-en), (man) servant, son, young man.<br />|3816|
παίω|pa�w|qaiz|1) to strike, smite<br />2) to sting (to strike or wound with a sting)<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb|<b>G3817</b><br />A primary verb; to <i>hit</i> (as if by a single blow and less violently than G5180); specifically to <i>sting</i> (as a scorpion): - smite, strike.<br />|3817|
Πάκατιανή|P�katian�|qakatiang|Phrygia = "dry, barren"<br />1) in the 4th century after Christ, Phrygia was divided into Phrygia Salutaris and Phrygia Pacatiana; Laodicea was a city in the latter<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adjective|<b>G3818</b><br />Feminine of an adjective of uncertain derivation; <i>Pacatianian</i>, a section of Phrygia: - Pacatiana.<br />|3818|
πάλαι|p�lai|qalai|1) of old, former<br />2) long ago<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adverb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:717, 769|<b>G3819</b><br />Probably another form for G3825 (through the idea of <i>retrocession</i>); (adverbially) <i>formerly</i>, or (relatively) <i>sometime since</i>; (elliptically as adjective) <i>ancient:</i> - any while, a great while ago, (of) old, in time past.<br />|3819|
παλαιός|palai�j|qalaips|1) old, ancient<br />2) no longer new, worn by use, the worse for wear, old<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adjective<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:717, 769|<b>G3820</b><br />From G3819; <i>antique</i>, that is, <i>not recent</i>, <i>worn out:</i> - old.<br />|3820|
παλαιότης|palai�thj|qalaiptgs|1) oldness: the old state of life controlled by 'the letter'<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:720, 769|<b>G3821</b><br />From G3820; <i>antiquatedness:</i> - oldness.<br />|3821|
παλαιόω|palai�w|qalaipz|1) to make ancient or old<br />1a) to become old, to be worn out<br />1b) of things worn out by time and use<br />2) to declare a thing to be old and so about to be abrogated<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:720, 769|<b>G3822</b><br />From G3820; to <i>make</i> (passively <i>become</i>) <i>worn out</i>, or <i>declare obsolete:</i> - decay, make (wax) old.<br />|3822|
πάλη|p�lh|qalg|1) wrestling (a contest between two in which each endeavours to throw the other, and which is decided when the victor is able to hold his opponent down with his hand upon his neck)<br />1a) the term is transferred to the Christian's struggle with the power of evil<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:721, 770|<b>G3823</b><br />From <span class="uni">πάλλω</span> pall&ocirc; (to <i>vibrate</i>; another form for G906); <i>wrestling:</i> - + wrestle.<br />|3823|
παλιγγενεσία|paliggenes�a|qaliccenesia|1) new birth, reproduction, renewal, recreation, regeneration<br />1a) hence renovation, regeneration, the production of a new life consecrated to God, a radical change of mind for the better. The word often used to denote the restoration of a thing to its pristine state, its renovation, as a renewal or restoration of life after death<br />1b) the renovation of the earth after the deluge<br />1c) the renewal of the world to take place after its destruction by fire, as the Stoics taught<br />1d) the signal and glorious change of all things (in heaven and earth) for the better, that restoration of the primal and perfect condition of things which existed before the fall of our first parents, which the Jews looked for in connection with the advent of the Messiah, and which Christians expected in connection with the visible return of Jesus from heaven.<br />1e) other uses<br />1e1) of Cicero's restoration to rank and fortune on his recall from exile<br />1e2) of the restoration of the Jewish nation after exile<br />1e3) of the recovery of knowledge by recollection<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 1:686, 117|<b>G3824</b><br />From G3825 and G1078; (spiritual) <i>rebirth</i> (the state or the act), that is, (figuratively) spiritual <i>renovation</i>; specifically Messianic <i>restoration:</i> - regeneration.<br />|3824|
πάλιν|p�lin|qalin|1) anew, again<br />1a) renewal or repetition of the action<br />1b) again, anew<br />2) again, i.e. further, moreover<br />3) in turn, on the other hand<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adverb|<b>G3825</b><br />Probably from the same as G3823 (through the idea of <i>oscillatory</i> repetition); (adverbially) <i>anew</i>, that is, (of place) <i>back</i>, (of time) <i>once more</i>, or (conjugationally) <i>furthermore</i> or <i>on the other hand:</i> - again.<br />|3825|
παμπληθεί|pamplhqe�|qamqlghei|1) with the whole multitude<br />2) all together, one and all<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adverb|<b>G3826</b><br />Dative case (adverb) of a compound of G3956 and G4128; <i>in full multitude</i>, that is, <i>concertedly</i> or <i>simultaneously:</i> - all at once.<br />|3826|
πάμπολυς|p�mpoluj|qamqplus|1) very great<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adjective|<b>G3827</b><br />From G3956 and G4183; <i>full many</i>, that is, <i>immense:</i> - very great.<br />|3827|
Παμφυλία|Pamful�a|qamvulia|Pamphylia = "of every tribe"<br />1) a province in Asia Minor, bounded on the east by Cilicia, on the west by Lycia and Phrygia Minor, on the north by Galatia and Cappadocia, and on the south by the Mediterranean Sea<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun proper locative<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 4:263, 533|<b>G3828</b><br />From a compound of G3956 and G5443; <i>every tribal</i>, that is, <i>heterogeneous</i> (G5561 being implied); <i>Pamphylia</i>, a region of Asia Minor: - Pamphylia.<br />|3828|
πανδοχεῖον|pandoce�on|qandpxeipn|1) an inn, a public house for the reception of strangers<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter|<b>G3829</b><br />Neuter of a presumed compound of G3956 and a derivative of G1209; <i>all receptive</i>, that is, a public <i>lodging</i> place (<i>caravanserai</i> or <i>khan</i>): - inn.<br />|3829|
πανδοχεύς|pandoce�j|qandpxeus|1) an inn keeper, host<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine|<b>G3830</b><br />From the same as G3829; an <i>innkeeper</i> (<i>warden of a caravanserai</i>): - host.<br />|3830|
πανήγυρις|pan�gurij|qangcuris|1) a festal gathering of the whole people to celebrate public games or other solemnities<br />2) a public festal assembly<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:722, 770|<b>G3831</b><br />From G3956 and a derivative of G58; a <i>mass meeting</i>, that is, (figuratively) <i>universal companionship:</i> - general assembly.<br />|3831|
πανοικεί|panoike�|qanpikei|1) with all (his) house, with (his) whole family<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adverb|<b>G3832</b><br />Adverb from G3956 and G3624; <i>with</i> the <i>whole family:</i> - with all his house.<br />|3832|
πανοπλία|panopl�a|qanpqlia|1) full armour, complete armour<br />1a) includes shield, sword, lance, helmet, greaves, and breastplate<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:295, 702|<b>G3833</b><br />From a compound of G3956 and G3696; <i>full armor</i> ("panoply"): - all (whole) armour.<br />|3833|
πανουργία|panourg�a|qanpurcia|1) craftiness, cunning<br />2) a specious or false wisdom<br />3) in a good sense, prudence, skill, in undertaking and carrying on affairs<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:722, 770|<b>G3834</b><br />From G3835; <i>adroitness</i>, that is, (in a bad sense) <i>trickery</i> or <i>sophistry:</i> - (cunning) craftiness, subtilty.<br />|3834|
πανοῦργος|pano�rgoj|qanpurcps|1) skilful, clever<br />1a) in a good sense, fit to undertake and accomplish anything, dexterous, wise, sagacious, skilful<br />1b) in a bad sense, crafty, cunning, knavish, treacherous, deceitful<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adjective<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:722, 770|<b>G3835</b><br />From G3956 and G2041; <i>all working</i>, that is, <i>adroit</i> (<i>shrewd</i>): - crafty.<br />|3835|
πανταχῇ|pantac�|qantaxg|1) from all sides, from every quarter<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adverb|<b>G3836</b><br />Adverb (of <i>source</i>) from G3837; <i>from all</i> directions: - from every quarter.<br />|3836|
πανταχοῦ|pantaco�|qantaxpu|1) everywhere<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adverb|<b>G3837</b><br />Genitive case (as adverb of <i>place</i>) of a presumed derivative of G3956; <i>universally:</i> - in all places, everywhere.<br />|3837|
παντελής|pantel�j|qantelgs|1) all complete, perfect<br />2) completely, perfectly, utterly<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adjective<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 8:66, 1161|<b>G3838</b><br />From G3956 and G5056; <i>full ended</i>, that is, <i>entire</i> (neuter as noun, <i>completion</i>): -  + in [no] wise, uttermost.<br />|3838|
