πρωτοτόκια|prwtot�kia|qrztptpkia|1) the right or advantages of the first born son<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 6:871, 965|<b>G4415</b><br />From G4416; <i>primogeniture</i> (as a privilege): - birthright.<br />|4415|
πρωτότοκος|prwt�tokoj|qrztptpkps|1) the firstborn<br />1a) of man or beast<br />1b) of Christ, the first born of all creation<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adjective<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 6:871, 965|<b>G4416</b><br />From G4413 and the alternate of G5088; <i>first born</i> (usually as noun, literally or figuratively): - firstbegotten (-born).<br />|4416|
πταίω|pta�w|qtaiz|1) to cause one to stumble or fall<br />2) to stumble<br />2a) to err, make a mistake, to sin<br />2b) to fall into misery, become wretched<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 6:883, 968|<b>G4417</b><br />A form of G4098; to <i>trip</i>, that is, (figuratively) to <i>err</i>, <i>sin</i>, <i>fail</i> (of salvation): - fall, offend, stumble.<br />|4417|
πτέρνα|pt�rna|qterna|1) the heel (of the foot)<br />2) to lift up the heel against one<br />2a) to injure one by trickery (figure borrowed either from kicking, or from a wrestler tripping up his antagonist)<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine|<b>G4418</b><br />Of uncertain derivation; the <i>heel</i> (figuratively): - heel.<br />|4418|
