πτωχεύω|ptwce�w|qtzxeuz|1) to be a beggar, to beg, to be poor<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 6:885, 969|<b>G4433</b><br />From G4434; to <i>be a beggar</i>, that is, (by implication) to <i>become indigent</i> (figuratively): - become poor.<br />|4433|
πτωχός|ptwc�j|qtzxps|1) reduced to beggary, begging, asking alms<br />2) destitute of wealth, influence, position, honour<br />2a) lowly, afflicted, destitute of the Christian virtues and eternal riches<br />2b) helpless, powerless to accomplish an end<br />2c) poor, needy<br />3) lacking in anything<br />3a) as respects their spirit<br />3a1) destitute of wealth of learning and intellectual culture which the schools afford (men of this class most readily give themselves up to Christ's teaching and proved them selves fitted to lay hold of the heavenly treasure)<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adjective<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 6:885, 969|<b>G4434</b><br />From <span class="uni">πτώσσω</span> pt&ocirc;ss&ocirc; (to <i>crouch</i>; akin to G4422 and the alternate of G4098); a <i>beggar</i> (as <i>cringing</i>), that is, <i>pauper</i> (strictly denoting absolute or public <i>mendicancy</i>, although also used in a qualified or relative sense; whereas G3993 properly means only <i>straitened</i> circumstances in private), literally (often as noun) or figuratively (<i>distressed</i>): - beggar (-ly), poor.<br />|4434|
