σκηνοπηγία|skhnophg�a|skgnpqgcia|1) the construction of a tabernacle or tabernacles<br />2) the feast of tabernacles; this festival was observed by the Jews yearly for seven days, beginning with the 15th of the month Tisri<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 7:390, 1040|<b>G4634</b><br />From G4636 and G4078; the <i>Festival of Tabernacles</i> (so called from the custom of erecting booths for temporary homes): - tabernacles.<br />|4634|
σκηνοποιός|skhnopoi�j|skgnpqpips|1) a tentmaker<br />2) one that makes small portable tents, of leather or cloth of goat's hair or linen, for the use of travellers<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adjective<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 7:393, 1040|<b>G4635</b><br />From G4633 and G4160; a <i>manufacturer of tents:</i> - tentmaker.<br />|4635|
σκῆνος|sk�noj|skgnps|1) a tabernacle, a tent<br />2) metaphorically of the human body, in which the soul dwells as in a tent, and which is taken down at death<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 7:381, 1040|<b>G4636</b><br />From G4633; a <i>hut</i> or temporary residence, that is, (figuratively) the human <i>body</i> (as the abode of the spirit): - tabernacle.<br />|4636|
σκηνόω|skhn�w|skgnpz|1) to fix one's tabernacle, have one's tabernacle, abide (or live) in a tabernacle (or tent), tabernacle<br />2) to dwell<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 7:385, 1040|<b>G4637</b><br />From G4636; to <i>tent</i> or <i>encamp</i>, that is, (figuratively) to <i>occupy</i> (as a mansion) or (specifically) to <i>reside</i> (as God did in the Tabernacle of old, a symbol fo protection and communion): - dwell.<br />|4637|
σκήνωμα|sk�nwma|skgnzma|1) a tent, a tabernacle<br />1a) of the temple as God's habitation<br />1b) of the tabernacle of the covenant<br />1c) metaphorically of the human body as the dwelling of the soul<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 7:383, 1040|<b>G4638</b><br />From G4637; an <i>encampment</i>, that is, (figuratively) the <i>Temple</i> (as God's residence), the <i>body</i> (as a tenement for the soul): - tabernacle.<br />|4638|
