σπλαγχνίζομαι|splagcn�zomai|sqlacxnifpmai|1) to be moved as to one's bowels, hence to be moved with compassion, have compassion (for the bowels were thought to be the seat of love and pity)<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 7:548, 1067|<b>G4697</b><br />Middle voice from G4698; to have the <i>bowels</i> yearn, that is, (figuratively) <i>feel sympathy</i>, to <i>pity:</i> - have (be moved with) compassion.<br />|4697|
σπλάγχνον|spl�gcnon|sqlacxnpn|1) bowels, intestines, (the heart, lungs, liver, etc.)<br />1a) bowels<br />1b) the bowels were regarded as the seat of the more violent passions, such as anger and love; but by the Hebrews as the seat of the tenderer affections, especially kindness, benevolence, compassion; hence our heart (tender mercies, affections, etc.)<br />1c) a heart in which mercy resides<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 7:548, 1067|<b>G4698</b><br />Probably strengthened from <span class="uni">σπλήν; spl&ecirc;n</span><br />(the "spleen"); an <i>intestine</i> (plural); figuratively <i>pity</i> or <i>sympathy:</i> - bowels, inward affection, + tender mercy.<br />|4698|
