στάδιον|st�dion|stadipn|1) a space or distance of about 600 feet (185 m)<br />2) a race course<br />2a) place in which contests in running were held, the one who outstripped the rest and reached the goal first, receiving the prize. Courses of this description were found in most of the larger Greek cities, and were like that at Olympia, 600 Greek feet in length<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter|<b>G4712</b><br />Or the masculine plural form, <span class="uni">στάδιος</span> stadios <i>stad'-ee-os</i>. <br />From the base of G2476 (as <i>fixed</i>); a <i>stade</i> or certain measure of distance; by implication a <i>stadium</i> or <i>race course:</i> - furlong, race.<br />|4712|
στάμνος|st�mnoj|stamnps|1) among the Greeks an earthen jar, into which wine was drawn off for keeping but also used for other purposes<br />1a) of the little jar in which the manna was kept, laid up in the ark of the covenant<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine|<b>G4713</b><br />From the base of G2476 (as <i>stationary</i>); a <i>jar</i> or earthen <i>tank:</i> - pot.<br />|4713|
στάσις|st�sij|stasis|1) a standing, station, state<br />2) an insurrection<br />3) strife, insurrection<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 7:568, 1070|<b>G4714</b><br />From the base of G2476; a <i>standing</i> (properly the act), that is, (by analogy) <i>position</i> (<i>existence</i>); by implication a popular <i>uprising</i>; figuratively <i>controversy:</i> - dissension, insurrection, X standing, uproar.<br />|4714|
στατήρ|stat�r|statgr|1) a stater, a coin<br />1a) in the NT a silver stater equal to four Attic or two Alexandrian drachmas, a Jewish shekel<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine|<b>G4715</b><br />From the base of G2746; a <i>stander</i> (<i>standard</i> of value), that is, (specifically) a <i>stater</i> or certain coin: - piece of money.<br />|4715|
σταυρός|staur�j|staurps|1) an upright stake, especially a pointed one<br />2) a cross<br />2a) a well known instrument of most cruel and ignominious punishment, borrowed by the Greeks and Romans from the Phoenicians; to it were affixed among the Romans, down to the time of Constantine the Great, the guiltiest criminals, particularly the basest slaves, robbers, the authors and abetters of insurrections, and occasionally in the provinces, at the arbitrary pleasure of the governors, upright and peaceable men also, and even Roman citizens themselves<br />2b) the crucifixion which Christ underwent<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 7:572, 1071|<b>G4716</b><br />From the base of G2476; a <i>stake</i> or <i>post</i> (as <i>set</i> upright), that is, (specifically) a <i>pole</i> or <i>cross</i> (as an instrument of capital punishment); figuratively <i>exposure to death</i>, that is, <i>self denial</i>; by implication the <i>atonement</i> of Christ: - cross.<br />|4716|
σταυρόω|staur�w|staurpz|1) to stake, drive down stakes<br />2) to fortify with driven stakes, to palisade<br />3) to crucify<br />3a) to crucify one<br />3b) metaphorically to crucify the flesh, destroy its power utterly (the nature of the figure implying that the destruction is attended with intense pain)<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 7:581, 1071|<b>G4717</b><br />From G4716; to <i>impale</i> on the cross; figuratively to <i>extinguish</i> (<i>subdue</i>) passion or selfishness: - crucify.<br />|4717|
σταφυλή|staful�|stavulg|1) grapes, bunch of grapes<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine|<b>G4718</b><br />Probably from the base of G4735; a <i>cluster</i> of grapes (as if <i>intertwined</i>): - grapes.<br />|4718|
στάχυς|st�cuj|staxus|1) an ear of corn or of growing grain<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine|<b>G4719</b><br />From the base of G2476; a <i>head</i> of grain (as <i>standing</i> out from the stalk): - ear (of corn).<br />|4719|
Στάχυς|St�cuj|staxus|Stachys = "a head of grain"<br />1) a man with a Greek name, he is said to be one of the seventy disciples, and bishop of Byzantium (Gill)<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun proper masculine|<b>G4720</b><br />The same as G4719; <i>Stachys</i>, a Christian: - Stachys.<br />|4720|
στέγη|st�gh|stecg|1) a roof: of a house<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine|<b>G4721</b><br />Strengthened from a primary word <span class="uni">τέγος</span> tegos (a "thatch" or "deck" of building); a <i>roof:</i> - roof.<br />|4721|
στέγω|st�gw|stecz|1) deck, thatch, to cover<br />1a) to protect or keep by covering, to preserve<br />2) to cover over with silence<br />2a) to keep secret<br />2b) to hide, conceal<br />2b1) of the errors and faults of others<br />3) by covering to keep off something which threatens, to bear up against, hold out against, and so endure, bear, forbear<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 7:585, 1073|<b>G4722</b><br />From G4721; to <i>roof</i> over, that is, (figuratively) to <i>cover</i> with silence (<i>endure</i> patiently): - (for-) bear, suffer.<br />|4722|
