σῶμα|s�ma|szma|1) the body both of men or animals<br />1a) a dead body or corpse<br />1b) the living body<br />1b1) of animals<br />2) the bodies of planets and of stars (heavenly bodies)<br />3) is used of a (large or small) number of men closely united into one society, or family as it were; a social, ethical, mystical body<br />3a) so in the NT of the church<br />4) that which casts a shadow as distinguished from the shadow itself<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 7:1024, 1140|<b>G4983</b><br />From G4982; the <i>body</i> (as a <i>sound</i> whole), used in a very wide application, literally or figuratively: - bodily, body, slave.<br />|4983|
