Φαρισαῖος|Farisa�oj|varisaips|1) A sect that seems to have started after the Jewish exile. In addition to OT books the Pharisees recognised in oral tradition a standard of belief and life. They sought for distinction and praise by outward observance of external rites and by outward forms of piety, and such as ceremonial washings, fastings, prayers, and alms giving; and, comparatively negligent of genuine piety, they prided themselves on their fancied good works. They held strenuously to a belief in the existence of good and evil angels, and to the expectation of a Messiah; and they cherished the hope that the dead, after a preliminary experience either of reward or of penalty in Hades, would be recalled to life by him, and be requited each according to his individual deeds. In opposition to the usurped dominion of the Herods and the rule of the Romans, they stoutly upheld the theocracy and their country's cause, and possessed great influence with the common people. According to Josephus they numbered more than 6000. They were bitter enemies of Jesus and his cause; and were in turn severely rebuked by him for their avarice, ambition, hollow reliance on outward works, and affection of piety in order to gain popularity.<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 9:11, 1246|<b>G5330</b><br />Of Hebrew origin (compare [H6567]); a <i>separatist</i>, that is, exclusively <i>religious</i>; a <i>Pharisaean</i>, that is, Jewish sectary: - Pharisee.<br />|5330|
φαρμακεία|farmake�a|varmakeia|1) the use or the administering of drugs<br />2) poisoning<br />3) sorcery, magical arts, often found in connection with idolatry and fostered by it<br />4) metaphorically the deceptions and seductions of idolatry<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine|<b>G5331</b><br />From G5332; <i>medication</i> ("pharmacy"), that is, (by extension) <i>magic</i> (literal or figurative): - sorcery, witchcraft.<br />|5331|
φάρμακον|f�rmakon|varmakpn|1) one who prepares or uses magical remedies<br />2) sorcerer<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine|<b>G5332</b><br />From <span class="uni">φάρμακον</span> pharmakon (a <i>drug</i>, that is, spell giving <i>potion</i>); a <i>druggist</i> ("pharmacist") or <i>poisoner</i>, that is, (by extension) a <i>magician:</i> - sorcerer.<br />|5332|
φάρμακος|f�rmakoj|varmakps|1) pertaining to magical arts<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adjective|<b>G5333</b><br />The same as G5332: - sorcerer.<br />|5333|
φάσις|f�sij|vasis|1) in the Attic orators, the exposure of (informing against) those who have embezzled the property of the state, or violated the laws respecting the importation or exporting of merchandise, or defrauded their wards<br />2) the disclosure of secret crime<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine|<b>G5334</b><br />From G5346 (not the same as "phase", which is from G5316); a <i>saying</i>, that is, <i>report:</i> - tidings.<br />|5334|
φάσκω|f�skw|vaskz|1) to affirm, allege, portend or profess<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb|<b>G5335</b><br />Prolongation from the same as G5346; to <i>assert:</i> - affirm, profess, say.<br />|5335|
φάτνη|f�tnh|vatng|1) a crib, a manger<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 9:49, 1251|<b>G5336</b><br />From <span class="uni">πατέομαι</span> pateomai (to <i>eat</i>); a <i>crib</i> (for fodder): - manger, stall.<br />|5336|
φαῦλος|fa�loj|vaulps|1) easy, slight, ordinary, mean, worthless, of no account<br />2) ethically, bad, base, wicked<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adjective|<b>G5337</b><br />Apparently a primary word; "foul" or "flawy", that is, (figuratively) <i>wicked:</i> - evil.<br />|5337|
φέγγος|f�ggoj|veccps|1) light<br />1a) of the moon<br />1b) of a candle or lamp<br />2) the bright sunshine, the beam of light<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun neuter|<b>G5338</b><br />Probably akin to the base of G5457 (compare G5350); <i>brilliancy:</i> - light.<br />|5338|
φείδομαι|fe�domai|veidpmai|1) to spare<br />2) to abstain<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb|<b>G5339</b><br />Of uncertain affinity; to <i>be chary</i> of, that is, (subjectively) to <i>abstain</i> or (objectively) to <i>treat leniently:</i> - forbear, spare.<br />|5339|
φειδομένως|feidom�nwj|veidpmenzs|1) sparingly<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adverb|<b>G5340</b><br />Adverb from participle of G5339; <i>abstemiously</i>, that is, <i>stingily:</i> - sparingly.<br />|5340|
φαιλόνης|fail�nhj|vailpngs|1) a travelling cloke, used for protection against stormy weather<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine|<b>G5341</b><br />By transposition for a derivation probably of G5316 (as <i>showing</i> outside the other garments); a <i>mantle</i> (<i>surtout</i>): - cloke.<br />|5341|
φέρω|f�rw|verz|1) to carry<br />1a) to carry some burden<br />1a1) to bear with one's self<br />1b) to move by bearing; move or, to be conveyed or borne, with the suggestion of force or speed<br />1b1) of persons borne in a ship over the sea<br />1b2) of a gust of wind, to rush<br />1b3) of the mind, to be moved inwardly, prompted<br />1c) to bear up, i.e. uphold (keep from falling)<br />1c1) of Christ, the preserver of the universe<br />2) to bear, i.e. endure, to endure the rigour of a thing, to bear patiently one's conduct, or spare one (abstain from punishing or destroying)<br />3) to bring, bring to, bring forward<br />3a) to move to, apply<br />3b) to bring in by announcing, to announce<br />3c) to bear, i.e. bring forth, produce; to bring forward in a speech<br />3d) to lead, conduct<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 9:56, 1252|<b>G5342</b><br />A primary verb (for which other and apparently not cognate ones are used in certain tenses only; namely <span class="uni">οἴω</span> oi&ocirc; and <span class="uni">ἐνέγκω</span> enegk&ocirc; to "bear" or <i>carry</i> (in a very wide application, literally and figuratively: - be, bear, bring (forth), carry, come, + let her drive, be driven, endure, go on, lay, lead, move, reach, rushing, uphold.<br />|5342|
φεύγω|fe�gw|veucz|1) to flee away, seek safety by flight<br />2) metaphorically to flee (to shun or avoid by flight) something abhorrent, especially vices<br />3) to be saved by flight, to escape safely out of danger<br />4) poetically, to flee away, vanish<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb|<b>G5343</b><br />Apparently a primary verb; to <i>run away</i> (literally or figuratively); by implication to <i>shun</i>; by analogy to <i>vanish:</i> - escape, flee (away).<br />|5343|
Φῆλιξ|F�lix|vglio|Felix = "happy"<br />1) a Roman procurator of Judea appointed by the emperor Claudius in A.D. 53. He ruled the province in a mean, cruel, and profligate manner. His period of office was full of troubles and seditions. Paul was brought before Felix at Caesarea. He was remanded in prison, and kept there two years in hopes of extorting money from him. Act 24:26,Act 24:27. At the end of that time Porcius Festus was appointed to supersede Felix, who, on his return to Rome, was accused by the Jews in Caesarea, and would have suffered the penalty due to his atrocities had not his brother Pallas prevailed with the emperor Nero to spare him. The wife of Felix was Drusilla, daughter of Herod Agrippa I., who was his third wife and whom he persuaded to leave her husband and marry him.<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun proper masculine|<b>G5344</b><br />Of Latin origin; <i>happy</i>; <i>Phelix</i> (that is, <i>Felix</i>), a Roman: - Felix.<br />|5344|
φήμη|f�mh|vgmg|1) fame, report<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine|<b>G5345</b><br />From G5346; a <i>saying</i>, that is, <i>rumor</i> ("fame"): - fame.<br />|5345|
φημί|fhm�|vgmi|1) to make known one's thoughts, to declare<br />2) to say<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb|<b>G5346</b><br />Properly the same as the base of G5457 and G5316; to <i>show</i> or <i>make known</i> one's thoughts, that is, <i>speak</i> or <i>say</i>: - affirm, say. Compare G3004.<br />|5346|
Φῆστος|F�stoj|vgstps|[Porcius] Festus = "festival"<br />1) the successor of Felix as procurator of Judea<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun proper masculine|<b>G5347</b><br />Of Latin derivation; <i>festal</i>; <i>Phestus</i> (that is, <i>Festus</i>), a Roman: - Festus.<br />|5347|
φθάνω|fq�nw|vhanz|1) to come before, precede, anticipate<br />2) to come to, arrive at<br />3) to reach, attain to<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 9:88, 1258|<b>G5348</b><br />Apparently a primary verb; to <i>be beforehand</i>, that is, <i>anticipate</i> or <i>precede</i>; by extension to <i>have arrived</i> at: - (already) attain, come, prevent.<br />|5348|
φθαρτός|fqart�j|vhartps|1) corruptible, perishing<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adjective<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 9:93, 1259|<b>G5349</b><br />From G5351; <i>decayed</i>, that is, (by implication) <i>perishable:</i> - corruptible.<br />|5349|
φθέγγομαι|fq�ggomai|vheccpmai|1) to give out a sound, noise or cry<br />1a) of any sort of sound or voice, whether of man or of animal or inanimate object as thunder, musical instruments, etc.<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb|<b>G5350</b><br />Probably akin to G5338 and thus to G5346; to <i>utter</i> a clear sound, that is, (genitive case) to <i>proclaim:</i> - speak.<br />|5350|
φθείρω|fqe�rw|vheirz|1) to corrupt, to destroy<br />1a) in the opinion of the Jews, the temple was corrupted or "destroyed" when anyone defiled or in the slightest degree damaged anything in it, or if its guardians neglected their duties<br />1b) to lead away a Christian church from that state of knowledge and holiness in which it ought to abide<br />1c) to be destroyed, to perish<br />1d) in an ethical sense, to corrupt, deprave<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 9:93, 1259|<b>G5351</b><br />Probably strengthened from <span class="uni">φθίω</span> phthi&ocirc; (to <i>pine</i> or <i>waste</i>): properly to <i>shrivel</i> or <i>wither</i>, that is, to <i>spoil</i> (by any process) or (genitive) to <i>ruin</i> (especially figuratively by moral influences, to <i>deprave</i>): - corrupt (self), defile, destroy.<br />|5351|
φθινοπωρινός|fqinopwrin�j|vhinpqzrinps|1) autumn trees<br />1a) trees such as they are at the close of autumn, dry, leafless and without fruit<br />1b) metaphorically of unfruitful, worthless men<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adjective|<b>G5352</b><br />From a derivative of <span class="uni">φθίνω</span> phthin&ocirc; (to <i>wane;</i> akin to the base of G5351) and G3703 (meaning <i>late autumn</i>) <i>autumnal</i> (as <i>stripped</i> of leaves): - whose fruit withereth.<br />|5352|
φθόγγος|fq�ggoj|vhpccps|1) musical sound, whether vocal or instrumental<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine|<b>G5353</b><br />From G5350; <i>utterance</i>, that is, a <i>musical</i> note (vocal or instrumental): - sound.<br />|5353|
φθονέω|fqon�w|vhpnez|1) to envy<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb|<b>G5354</b><br />From G5355; to be <i>jealous</i> of: - envy.<br />|5354|
φθόνος|fq�noj|vhpnps|1) envy<br />2) for envy, i.e. prompted by envy<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun masculine|<b>G5355</b><br />Probably akin to the base of G5351; <i>ill will</i> (as <i>detraction</i>), that is, <i>jealousy</i> (<i>spite</i>): - envy.<br />|5355|
φθορά|fqor�|vhpra|1) corruption, destruction, perishing<br />1a) that which is subject to corruption, what is perishable<br />1b) in the Christian sense, eternal misery in hell<br />2) in the NT, in an ethical sense, corruption, i.e. moral decay<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 9:93, 1259|<b>G5356</b><br />From G5351; <i>decay</i>, that is, <i>ruin</i> (spontaneous or inflicted, literally or figuratively): - corruption, destroy, perish.<br />|5356|
φιάλη|fi�lh|vialg|1) a broad shallow bowl, deep saucer<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine|<b>G5357</b><br />Of uncertain affinity; a broad shallow <i>cup</i> ("phial"): - vial.<br />|5357|
φιλάγαθος|fil�gaqoj|vilacahps|1) loving goodness<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adjective<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 1:18, 3|<b>G5358</b><br />From G5384 and G18; <i>fond of good</i>, that is, a <i>promoter of virtue:</i> - love of good men.<br />|5358|
Φιλαδέλφεια|Filad�lfeia|viladelveia|Philadelphia = "brotherly love"<br />1) a city of Lydia in Asia Minor, situated near the eastern base of of Mount Tmolus, founded and named by the Pergamene king, Attalus II Philadelphus. After the death of Attalus III Philometor, 133 BC, it together with his entire kingdom came by his will under the jurisdiction of the Romans.<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun proper locative<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 1:144,*|<b>G5359</b><br />From <span class="uni">Φιλάδέλφος</span> Philadelphos (the same as G5361), a king of Pergamos; <i>Philadelphia</i>, a place in Asia Minor: - Philadelphia.<br />|5359|
φιλαδελφία|filadelf�a|viladelvia|1) love of brothers or sisters, brotherly love<br />2) in the NT the love which Christians cherish for each other as brethren<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 1:144, 22|<b>G5360</b><br />From G5361; <i>fraternal affection:</i> - brotherly love (kindness), love of the brethren.<br />|5360|
φιλάδελφος|fil�delfoj|viladelvps|1) loving brother or sister<br />2) in a broader sense, loving one like a brother, loving one's fellow countrymen<br />2a) of an Israelite<br />2b) of a Christian loving Christians<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adjective<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 1:144, 22|<b>G5361</b><br />From G5384 and G80; <i>fond of brethren</i>, that is, <i>fraternal:</i> - love as brethren.<br />|5361|
φίλανδρος|f�landroj|vilandrps|1) loving her husband<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adjective|<b>G5362</b><br />From G5384 and G435; <i>fond of man</i>, that is, <i>affectionate</i> as a wife: - love their husbands.<br />|5362|
φιλανθρωπία|filanqrwp�a|vilanhrzqia|1) love of mankind, benevolence<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 9:107, 1261|<b>G5363</b><br />From the same as G5364; <i>fondness of mankind</i>, that is, <i>benevolence</i> ("philanthropy"): - kindness, love towards man.<br />|5363|
φιλανθρώπως|filanqr�pwj|vilanhrzqzs|1) humanely, kindly<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adverb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 9:107, 1261|<b>G5364</b><br />Adverb from a compound of G5384 and G444; <i>fondly to man</i> (philanthropically), that is, <i>humanely:</i> - courteously.<br />|5364|
φιλαργυρία|filargur�a|vilarcuria|1) love of money, avarice<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine|<b>G5365</b><br />From G5366; <i>avarice:</i> - love of money.<br />|5365|
φιλάργυρος|fil�rguroj|vilarcurps|1) loving money, avarice<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adjective|<b>G5366</b><br />From G5384 and G696; <i>fond of silver</i> (<i>money</i>), that is, <i>avaricious:</i> - covetous.<br />|5366|
φίλαυτος|f�lautoj|vilautps|1) loving one's self<br />2) too intent on one's interest, selfish<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adjective|<b>G5367</b><br />From G5384 and G846; <i>fond of self</i>, that is, <i>selfish:</i> - lover of own self.<br />|5367|
φιλέω|fil�w|vilez|1) to love<br />1a) to approve of<br />1b) to like<br />1c) sanction<br />1d) to treat affectionately or kindly, to welcome, befriend<br />2) to show signs of love<br />2a) to kiss<br />3) to be fond of doing<br />3a) be wont, use to do<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> verb<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 9:114, 1262|<b>G5368</b><br />From G5384; to <i>be a friend to</i> (<i>fond of</i> [an individual or an object]), that is, <i>have affection</i> for (denoting <i>personal</i> attachment, as a matter of sentiment or feeling; while G25 is wider, embracing especially the judgment and the <i>deliberate</i> assent of the will as a matter of principle, duty and propriety: the two thus stand related very much as G2309 and G1014, or as G2372 and G3563 respectively; the former being chiefly of the <i>heart</i> and the latter of the <i>head</i>); specifically to <i>kiss</i> (as a mark of tenderness): - kiss, love.<br />|5368|
