Φιλιππήσιος|Filipp�sioj|viliqqgsips|1) a resident of the town of Philippi<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun proper masculine|<b>G5374</b><br />From G5375; a <i>Philippesian</i> (<i>Philippian</i>), that is, native of Philippi: - Philippian.<br />|5374|
Φίλιπποι|F�lippoi|viliqqpi|Philippi = "Lover of horses"<br />1) a city of Macedonia located on or near the northern coast of the Aegean Sea, between the rivers Strymon and Nestus, and the cities Neapolis and Amphipolis<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun proper locative|<b>G5375</b><br />Plural of G5376; <i>Philippi</i>, a place in Macedonia: - Philippi.<br />|5375|
Φίλιππος|F�lippoj|viliqqps|Philip = "lover of horses"<br />1) an apostle of Christ<br />2) an evangelist and one of the seven deacons of the Jerusalem church<br />3) tetrarch of Trachonitis, was brother to Herod Antipas, by the father's, but not by the mother's side. Philip was born of Cleopatra, of Jerusalem, and Herod of Malthace, a Samaritan: he died in the twentieth year of Tiberias, five years after his mention in Luk 3:1. He built Caesarea Philippi. His step brother Herod Antipas, married his wife unlawfully. (Gill)<br />4) see G2542, Caesarea Philippi<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun proper masculine|<b>G5376</b><br />From G5384 and G2462; <i>fond of horses</i>; <i>Philippus</i>, the name of four Israelites: - Philip.<br />|5376|
φιλόθεος|fil�qeoj|vilpheps|1) loving God<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adjective|<b>G5377</b><br />From G5384 and G2316; <i>fond of God</i>, that is, <i>pious:</i> - lover of God.<br />|5377|
Φιλόλογος|Fil�logoj|vilplpcps|Philologus = "lover of the Word"<br />1) a Christian Paul greets in his epistle to Romans<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun proper masculine|<b>G5378</b><br />From G5384 and G3056; <i>fond of words</i>, that is, <i>talkative</i> (<i>argumentative</i>, <i>learned</i>, "philological"); <i>Philologus</i>, a Christian: - Philologus.<br />|5378|
φιλονεικία|filoneik�a|vilpneikia|1) love of strife, eagerness to contend<br />2) contention<br />2a) in a good sense, emulation<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine|<b>G5379</b><br />From G5380; <i>quarrelsomeness</i>, that is, a <i>dispute:</i> - strife.<br />|5379|
φιλόνεικος|fil�neikoj|vilpneikps|1) fond of strife, contentious<br />1a) in a good sense, emulous<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> adjective|<b>G5380</b><br />From G5384 and <span class="uni">νεῖκος</span> neikos (a <i>quarrel</i>; probably akin to G3534); <i>fond of strife</i>, that is, <i>disputatious:</i> - contentious.<br />|5380|
φιλοξενία|filoxen�a|vilpoenia|1) love to strangers, hospitality<br /><b>Part of Speech:</b> noun feminine<br /><b>Citing in TDNT:</b> 5:1,*|<b>G5381</b><br />From G5382; <i>hospitableness:</i> - entertain strangers, hospitality.<br />|5381|
